==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sent- (feel)}}
From {{der|en|fro|sentement}}, from {{der|en|la|sentimentum}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛn.tɪ.mənt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-sentiment.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A general thought, [[feel]]ing, or sense.
#: ''The '''sentiment''' emerged that we were acting too soon.''
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|page=15|volume=II|passage="Now, my dear young friend," continued the bookseller, "you seem fond of reason; let me talk a little reason to you. Here, take your pamphlet again: there is good material in it, but it requires the making up. Leave out some of your arguments, and throw in a few '''sentiments''',—something about free-born Britons and wooden shoes! Englishmen like to have a few '''sentiments''' ready for after-dinner use, in case of a speech...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author={{w|Ben Travers}}
|chapter=5|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1521052W A Cuckoo in the Nest]
|passage=The departure was not unduly prolonged.{{...}}Within the door Mrs. Spoker hastily imparted to Mrs. Love a few final '''sentiments''' on the subject of Divine Intention in the disposition of buckets; farewells and last commiserations; a deep, guttural instigation to the horse; and the wheels of the waggonette crunched heavily away into obscurity.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} Feelings, especially tender feelings, as apart from reason or judgment, or of a weak or foolish kind.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=February|author=R. C. Riley|title=The London-Birmingham services - Past, Present and Future|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=99|text=To do the job thoroughly '''sentiment''' must be ignored and it seems inevitable that the famous Great Hall and the Doric Arch will have to be sacrificed to progress.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date= 3 March 2014|doi= 10.3390/rel5010219|author=Zoe Alderton|title= ‘Snapewives’ and ‘Snapeism’: A Fiction-Based Religion within the Harry Potter Fandom|work=Religions|publisher=MDPI|url= https://www.mdpi.com/2077-1444/5/1/219/htm |pages=219-257|volume=5|issue=1|passage=  Despite personal schisms and differences in spiritual experience, there is a very coherent theology of Snape shared between the wives. To examine this manifestation of religious fandom, I will first discuss the canon scepticism and anti-Rowling '''sentiment''' that helps to contextualise the wider belief in Snape as a character who extends beyond book and film.}}

